1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,461
Your phone is charging on the nightstand.

2
00:00:02,972 --> 00:00:05,062
Now follow that cable backward.

3
00:00:05,445 --> 00:00:08,318
Into the wall, out to the street,

4
00:00:08,318 --> 00:00:11,192
up the power lines, and keep going.

5
00:00:11,583 --> 00:00:16,854
For about 18 percent of America's utility-scale electricity, the Energy

6
00:00:16,854 --> 00:00:22,125
Information Administration says, this trip starts with a split atom.

7
00:00:22,512 --> 00:00:25,821
Today we follow that energy the whole way.

8
00:00:26,425 --> 00:00:29,582
And two things on this trip might surprise you.

9
00:00:30,024 --> 00:00:32,276
Roughly two-thirds of the energy never

10
00:00:32,276 --> 00:00:34,528
leaves the power plant as electricity.

11
00:00:34,911 --> 00:00:38,534
And the electrons at the plant never reach your phone.

12
00:00:38,928 --> 00:00:41,853
Here's the idea: your phone charger is the

13
00:00:41,853 --> 00:00:44,779
last link in a long chain of handoffs.

14
00:00:45,221 --> 00:00:47,633
Each step turns the energy into a

15
00:00:47,633 --> 00:00:49,702
form the next one can use.

16
00:00:50,542 --> 00:00:53,253
Inside a reactor, the fuel is

17
00:00:53,253 --> 00:00:55,964
uranium, formed into small ceramic pellets.

18
00:00:56,208 --> 00:00:59,155
The EIA says each pellet holds about as

19
00:00:59,155 --> 00:01:01,734
much energy as 150 gallons of oil.

20
00:01:01,943 --> 00:01:04,581
The pellets are stacked end to end

21
00:01:04,581 --> 00:01:06,842
in fuel rods 12 feet long.

22
00:01:07,219 --> 00:01:10,690
When a uranium-235 atom absorbs a slow-moving

23
00:01:10,690 --> 00:01:14,162
neutron, it splits into two smaller atoms.

24
00:01:14,673 --> 00:01:15,776
That's fission.

25
00:01:16,054 --> 00:01:19,751
It releases a lot of energy, plus extra neutrons that

26
00:01:19,751 --> 00:01:23,449
can split more atoms, which keeps a chain reaction going.

27
00:01:24,134 --> 00:01:26,589
The plant uses the heat from

28
00:01:26,589 --> 00:01:29,045
all that splitting to boil water.

29
00:01:29,433 --> 00:01:35,442
As of March 2026, the U.S. had 94 operating commercial reactors

30
00:01:35,442 --> 00:01:40,904
at 54 plants in 28 states, according to the EIA.

31
00:01:41,303 --> 00:01:42,510
And they run hard.

32
00:01:43,416 --> 00:01:47,311
In 2025, the average U.S. nuclear plant produced

33
00:01:47,311 --> 00:01:50,719
91 percent of its maximum possible output.

34
00:01:51,137 --> 00:01:54,626
The EIA calls that the capacity factor, and nuclear's

35
00:01:54,626 --> 00:01:58,114
was higher than any other type of power plant.

36
00:01:58,952 --> 00:02:01,704
Next, heat has to become motion.

37
00:02:02,214 --> 00:02:06,663
The Nuclear Regulatory Commission says only two reactor types

38
00:02:06,663 --> 00:02:11,112
make commercial power in the U.S., and about two-thirds

39
00:02:11,112 --> 00:02:15,067
of the licensed reactors are pressurized water reactors.

40
00:02:15,446 --> 00:02:18,454
In those, water under high pressure carries heat

41
00:02:18,454 --> 00:02:21,463
from the core to a steam generator, where

42
00:02:21,463 --> 00:02:24,095
it boils water in a separate loop.

43
00:02:24,699 --> 00:02:27,868
The other type, a boiling water reactor,

44
00:02:27,868 --> 00:02:30,585
makes steam right in the core.

45
00:02:30,973 --> 00:02:32,831
Either way, the steam is piped

46
00:02:32,831 --> 00:02:34,688
to a turbine and spins it.

47
00:02:34,967 --> 00:02:38,162
The turbine drives a generator, where a spinning

48
00:02:38,162 --> 00:02:40,958
magnet induces current in coils of wire.

49
00:02:41,654 --> 00:02:45,973
That's where motion finally becomes electricity.

50
00:02:46,370 --> 00:02:49,690
The generator doesn't spin at just any speed.

51
00:02:50,132 --> 00:02:53,986
In North America, the grid runs on alternating current

52
00:02:53,986 --> 00:02:57,840
at a standard 60 hertz, meaning 60 cycles every

53
00:02:57,840 --> 00:03:01,695
second, and the generators run in sync with it.

54
00:03:02,080 --> 00:03:04,332
And here's the first big loss.

55
00:03:04,715 --> 00:03:07,798
The EIA tracks how much heat a plant

56
00:03:07,798 --> 00:03:10,497
uses for each kilowatt-hour it sends out.

57
00:03:11,101 --> 00:03:14,624
For U.S. nuclear plants in 2025, that works

58
00:03:14,624 --> 00:03:18,148
out to an efficiency of about 33 percent.

59
00:03:18,547 --> 00:03:22,193
So roughly two-thirds of the heat never becomes electricity.

60
00:03:22,634 --> 00:03:25,952
The used steam is cooled back into water in a

61
00:03:25,952 --> 00:03:28,938
condenser, and that leftover heat has to go somewhere.

62
00:03:29,542 --> 00:03:31,480
Some plants use cooling towers.

63
00:03:31,724 --> 00:03:34,754
Others use a lake, a river, or the ocean.

64
00:03:35,145 --> 00:03:37,177
That's not unique to nuclear.

65
00:03:37,780 --> 00:03:42,748
Coal plants also run on steam, and in 2025 their

66
00:03:42,748 --> 00:03:47,219
average efficiency was about the same, around 32 percent.

67
00:03:48,066 --> 00:03:52,524
According to the Department of Energy, generators usually

68
00:03:52,524 --> 00:03:56,982
put out electricity at 5,000 to 34,500 volts.

69
00:03:57,679 --> 00:04:00,505
Think of voltage as electrical pressure, the

70
00:04:00,505 --> 00:04:02,927
push behind the flow of current.

71
00:04:03,306 --> 00:04:05,634
Right at the plant, a step-up transformer

72
00:04:05,634 --> 00:04:07,962
raises that pressure for the long haul.

73
00:04:08,449 --> 00:04:15,507
Typical U.S. transmission lines run at 138,000,

74
00:04:15,507 --> 00:04:21,557
230,000, 345,000, 500,000, or 765,000 volts.

75
00:04:21,942 --> 00:04:23,916
Why push the voltage that high?

76
00:04:24,357 --> 00:04:26,737
A transformer can't create power.

77
00:04:27,178 --> 00:04:29,609
So when it raises the voltage, the

78
00:04:29,609 --> 00:04:31,694
current drops by the same factor.

79
00:04:32,088 --> 00:04:34,825
And the energy a wire loses as heat

80
00:04:34,825 --> 00:04:37,220
depends on the square of the current.

81
00:04:37,730 --> 00:04:39,640
Cut the current in half, and

82
00:04:39,640 --> 00:04:41,550
that loss drops to a quarter.

83
00:04:42,154 --> 00:04:44,580
That's why the grid goes high.

84
00:04:44,976 --> 00:04:49,633
Or in the EIA's words, higher voltage makes

85
00:04:49,633 --> 00:04:53,707
long-distance transmission more efficient and less expensive.

86
00:04:54,553 --> 00:04:59,081
But you can't plug a lamp into 345,000 volts.

87
00:04:59,685 --> 00:05:03,386
So the way back down starts at a substation,

88
00:05:03,386 --> 00:05:06,674
where transformers lower the voltage for local lines.

89
00:05:07,050 --> 00:05:11,581
In one Department of Energy example, a substation takes

90
00:05:11,581 --> 00:05:16,112
power in at 34,000 volts and sends it out

91
00:05:16,112 --> 00:05:19,635
at 7,200 volts on neighborhood distribution lines.

92
00:05:20,018 --> 00:05:23,702
The final step-down happens close to your home: the

93
00:05:23,702 --> 00:05:27,386
can-shaped transformer on a utility pole, or a box

94
00:05:27,386 --> 00:05:31,071
on a concrete pad where the lines run underground.

95
00:05:31,470 --> 00:05:34,508
It feeds a standard U.S. house two

96
00:05:34,508 --> 00:05:37,112
120-volt hot wires plus a neutral.

97
00:05:37,681 --> 00:05:39,440
One of them gives you the

98
00:05:39,440 --> 00:05:41,199
120 volts at a regular outlet.

99
00:05:41,617 --> 00:05:45,267
Both together give you 240 volts for big loads

100
00:05:45,267 --> 00:05:48,513
like a clothes dryer or an electric range.

101
00:05:48,904 --> 00:05:52,654
Along that whole route, some energy is lost on the way.

102
00:05:52,933 --> 00:05:57,072
The EIA estimates that transmission and distribution losses

103
00:05:57,072 --> 00:06:01,211
averaged about 5 percent from 2018 through 2022.

104
00:06:01,907 --> 00:06:04,346
So the big loss happens back at the plant.

105
00:06:04,671 --> 00:06:07,620
The grid itself is fairly thrifty.

106
00:06:08,460 --> 00:06:10,120
Now for the second surprise.

107
00:06:10,445 --> 00:06:14,010
We say electricity flows, so it's easy to picture

108
00:06:14,010 --> 00:06:17,179
electrons racing from the plant to your house.

109
00:06:17,620 --> 00:06:19,327
They don't.

110
00:06:19,703 --> 00:06:23,218
In a copper wire, the average drift of electrons

111
00:06:23,218 --> 00:06:26,733
is only fractions of a millimeter per second, according

112
00:06:26,733 --> 00:06:29,467
to physics notes from Georgia State University.

113
00:06:30,164 --> 00:06:33,154
In one of their examples, it's less than

114
00:06:33,154 --> 00:06:35,771
a third of a millimeter per second.

115
00:06:36,170 --> 00:06:39,572
And on our grid, they don't even drift one way.

116
00:06:40,164 --> 00:06:44,455
With alternating current, the voltage swings positive, then

117
00:06:44,455 --> 00:06:48,210
negative, and back, 60 times every second.

118
00:06:48,895 --> 00:06:51,743
So the current keeps reversing, and the electrons

119
00:06:51,743 --> 00:06:54,235
mostly shuffle back and forth in place.

120
00:06:54,623 --> 00:06:56,759
Transformers break the chain, too.

121
00:06:57,003 --> 00:07:01,201
Inside one, energy crosses from one coil of wire to

122
00:07:01,201 --> 00:07:04,979
another through a magnetic field in an iron core.

123
00:07:05,370 --> 00:07:06,775
So what makes the trip?

124
00:07:07,158 --> 00:07:08,133
The energy.

125
00:07:08,307 --> 00:07:11,605
Push on one end of the line, and the push travels.

126
00:07:12,301 --> 00:07:16,295
Georgia State's notes put it this way: it's the change, the

127
00:07:16,295 --> 00:07:20,289
signal, that moves along wires close to the speed of light.

128
00:07:20,730 --> 00:07:25,804
Across a hundred miles, that's under a thousandth of a second.

129
00:07:26,650 --> 00:07:30,179
Which brings us back to the little box on your nightstand.

130
00:07:30,690 --> 00:07:35,132
Federal rules actually define it: an external power

131
00:07:35,132 --> 00:07:39,574
supply converts household electric current into DC current,

132
00:07:39,574 --> 00:07:43,461
or lower-voltage AC, to run a product.

133
00:07:43,849 --> 00:07:47,976
A battery charges when current is pushed through it one

134
00:07:47,976 --> 00:07:52,104
way, and wall current flips direction 120 times a second.

135
00:07:52,545 --> 00:07:56,325
So the charger lowers the voltage and turns AC

136
00:07:56,325 --> 00:07:59,685
into direct current, which flows in one direction.

137
00:08:00,081 --> 00:08:02,264
Then comes the final handoff.

138
00:08:03,192 --> 00:08:07,645
As the Department of Energy puts it, electricity has to be

139
00:08:07,645 --> 00:08:12,097
converted into chemical potential energy before it can be readily stored.

140
00:08:12,689 --> 00:08:17,713
While charging, electrons move through the circuit and ions move through

141
00:08:17,713 --> 00:08:22,279
the battery, and the energy ends up held in chemistry.

142
00:08:22,661 --> 00:08:24,391
And that's the end of the trip.

143
00:08:24,716 --> 00:08:27,811
Energy from a split uranium atom, now

144
00:08:27,811 --> 00:08:30,463
waiting as chemistry in your pocket.

145
00:08:31,298 --> 00:08:32,285
Quick recap.

146
00:08:32,714 --> 00:08:33,945
Fission makes heat.

147
00:08:34,189 --> 00:08:35,489
Heat makes steam.

148
00:08:35,988 --> 00:08:38,937
Steam spins a turbine, and the turbine

149
00:08:38,937 --> 00:08:41,886
drives a generator that makes 60-hertz power.

150
00:08:42,676 --> 00:08:46,027
In 2025, only about a third of

151
00:08:46,027 --> 00:08:48,899
a nuclear plant's heat became electricity.

152
00:08:49,281 --> 00:08:53,139
Transformers push the voltage up to hundreds of thousands of

153
00:08:53,139 --> 00:08:56,997
volts so the long trip loses less, then step it

154
00:08:56,997 --> 00:09:00,856
back down to 120 and 240 volts at your house.

155
00:09:01,530 --> 00:09:06,278
The electrons mostly stay put; the energy makes the trip.

156
00:09:06,663 --> 00:09:10,726
Here's my question for you: which step surprised you most?

157
00:09:11,110 --> 00:09:13,716
The heat that never becomes electricity, or

158
00:09:13,716 --> 00:09:16,322
the electrons that never leave your walls?

159
00:09:16,926 --> 00:09:18,691
Tell me in the comments.

160
00:09:19,081 --> 00:09:22,419
If you like seeing the hidden machinery behind everyday

161
00:09:22,419 --> 00:09:25,385
life, subscribe to Other End of the Wire.

162
00:09:25,989 --> 00:09:28,079
Every source behind the numbers in

163
00:09:28,079 --> 00:09:30,169
this video is in the description.

164
00:09:30,610 --> 00:09:32,038
Follow the electron.
